  <HOMEWORK title="PLL Bring-Up 4: It locks now, but the scenic route" prompt="The loop gains are back up near their normal magnitude and the loop does eventually report LOCKED - but a technician watching the output frequency during acquisition says it visibly overshoots and rings back and forth several times before settling, instead of approaching the target smoothly. Check whether the two loop gains are actually balanced against each other."
            hint="A PLL's damping comes from the BALANCE between the proportional and integral loop-filter gains, not either one alone. Is the proportional gain anywhere near its normal value relative to the integral gain, or is one path left dominating?"
            explanation="The integral gain is back at its normal value, but the proportional gain was left roughly 6x lower than normal - a classically underdamped loop filter (HOMEWORK_28 demonstrates the same fault in isolation). The integral path still has enough authority to eventually pull the frequency all the way in, but with too little proportional damping to restrain it, the loop overshoots and rings before settling rather than approaching smoothly. The fix is restoring the proportional gain to its normal value, which damps the ringing out without changing how far the loop can ultimately pull in."/>
</LABCHAIN>
